Z||000000||montage||07-19-2001||08:27 AM||||WWell after months of research on tires and wheels I decided to just get new tires and go through the front end with new KYB struts on the rear (fronts new, CFO wouldn't approve Racing Hart wheels at $500 / pop). I got Dunlop 8000 245/5015 and 225/50/15 (data shows these are at the top of the heap of street tires) with new ball joints, tie rods, rack bushing, upper control arm rubber bushings and new rear struts (not to mention I picked up a G-tech). I wound up breaking an upper control arm shaft (get torches a must in rust belt)and stripping a control arm mounting bolt. One thing I can say is poly bushings look much easier to install then rubber with rubber you have to get the sleeve out which is a @$$$*%&&$*(#9. I'm glad I did replace the upper bushings they were bad. I finally got it all together after over a week and my alignment guy was on vacation. So go to a place where the guy has 25 years and a new alignment machine. He does a great alignment as I specified with printout but charges me $149 (dealers only want $65 around here) but he did put 3 hours in it (all the bolts were free however). He writes on the bill after a test drive that the assend is wiggling under power. I noticed this before this all began but thought is was a bad strut (one was bad) and my Dunlop Graspic DS-1 snow tires that caused this. So I look under the car and find out that the frame mount for the passenger rear tie rod (toe in adjustment) is broke. It looks like one end of the bushing is pressed in and the other end is bolted. The mount on the press fit end is broken and the other end it holding it. I can't believe the alignment guy didn't see this. Well I need to fix this and would appreciate any ideas. I fortunately have friends with metal fabrication shops. Thanks for letting me vent a bit to those who understand the Fiero thing.||||reg||1
